
git clone         = Download repository for the first time
git remote add    = Connect local repo to GitHub
git pull          = Get latest changes from GitHub
git push          = Send local changes to GitHub

Scenario:
like in my local git, i created directory
mkdir Test
i have repo in github account -named mahesh.git
cd Test, i created few files test1, test2, test3
so now i want to push/add
so can i use git clone or git remote add ?

Example1 --(if the GitHub repo already has files) 
Ex: repo -> (mahesh.git) already contains a README or other files, do:
mkdir Test
cd Test
git init
git add .
git commit -m "Initial commit"
git remote add origin https://github.com/mahe0243/mahesh.git
git pull origin main --allow-unrelated-histories
git push origin main




Example2:
Why NOT git clone?

git clone is used when you want to download an existing repository from GitHub.

Ex: git clone https://github.com/mahe0243/mahesh.git

Git will create a new folder under your working directory 
mahesh/

Now you'll have:
Test/ <- your files
mahesh/ <- cloned repo

Your files inside Test are not automatically part of the cloned repo.
You would then need to copy them:

cp ../Test/* mahesh
git add .
git commit -m "Added files"
git push origin main

So for your case, cloning first is unnecessary.



